          "theme_heading": "Hatfield Oak to the Tower: Inheriting Bankruptcy and Cecil's Partnership (1558)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In November 1558, twenty-five-year-old Elizabeth Tudor ascended the English throne following the death of her Catholic half-sister Mary I. The young Queen inherited an impoverished, vulnerable realm burdened by an astronomical **£300,000** Crown debt, largely accumulated through costly Tudor wars in France and currency debasement that shook confidence in English coinage.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Because the Crown’s ordinary annual revenue stood at barely £286,000, royal treasurers were forced to take foreign loans on the **Antwerp** money market at steep 14% interest to fund basic expenditure. Seeking immediate administrative grip and fiscal stability, Elizabeth acted decisively on accession day by appointing Sir **William Cecil** as Principal Secretary, forging a political partnership that directed English statecraft for forty years.",
          "level_4": "Elizabeth became queen in 1558 facing a massive £300,000 debt left by Mary I. She had to borrow money at high interest from Antwerp and appointed William Cecil as her chief advisor to restore stability.",
