          "theme_heading": "Challenges of Gender & Marriage: Female Monarchical Rule, Legitimacy and Knox's Opposition (1558)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Elizabeth’s title was immediately questioned through sixteenth-century patriarchal prejudice against female rulers and religious doubts over her birth. Roman Catholic canon law maintained that Henry VIII’s marriage to Catherine of Aragon remained indissoluble, condemning his 1533 wedding to **Anne Boleyn** as invalid; devout Catholics therefore viewed Elizabeth as illegitimate and barred from inheriting the English crown.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Doubts were reinforced by Henry VIII’s own **1536 Succession Act**, passed after Anne Boleyn’s execution, which had legally declared Elizabeth illegitimate even after the 1544 Act restored her place in line. As an unmarried queen regnant, she faced relentless parliamentary pressure to marry and produce a **Protestant heir** lest sudden illness plunge England into dynastic civil war.",
