          "theme_heading": "Whitehall to the Shires: Reluctant Parliaments and Unpaid Country JPs (1559)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Beyond Whitehall, the Tudor Crown depended entirely upon unpaid county elites to enforce royal authority across the shires. Regional defence was entrusted to noble **Lords Lieutenant**, who mustered and trained county militias during emergencies, while daily local government rested on wealthy gentry appointed as **Justices of the Peace** to judge crimes, maintain roads, and administer poor laws.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Meanwhile, **Parliament** was not a permanent legislature but an occasional assembly summoned only when the monarch needed new statutes or emergency war funding. Although Elizabeth fiercely guarded her royal prerogative over marriage, religion, and foreign policy, constitutional custom dictated that extraordinary taxation required parliamentary consent, giving the House of Commons important financial leverage.",
