          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> In foreign affairs, Elizabeth inherited dangerous strategic encirclement by hostile Catholic powers. During Mary I’s disastrous French war alongside Habsburg Spain, England had suffered the humiliating loss of **Calais** in January 1558—its last continental foothold—damaging Tudor military prestige and leaving England’s southern coastline exposed to invasion fleets assembling across the narrow English Channel.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Danger was compounded to the north through France’s traditional **Auld Alliance** with Scotland, where veteran French garrison troops bolstered Regent Mary of Guise along the vulnerable English border. Her daughter, Catholic claimant **Mary, Queen of Scots**, was married to the French Dauphin Francis and quartered the English royal arms with her own, openly asserting her dynastic claim to Elizabeth’s throne.",
          "paragraphs": [
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> In foreign affairs, Elizabeth inherited dangerous strategic encirclement by hostile Catholic powers. During Mary I’s disastrous French war alongside Habsburg Spain, England had suffered the humiliating loss of **Calais** in January 1558—its last continental foothold—damaging Tudor military prestige and leaving England’s southern coastline exposed to invasion fleets assembling across the narrow English Channel.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Danger was compounded to the north through France’s traditional **Auld Alliance** with Scotland, where veteran French garrison troops bolstered Regent Mary of Guise along the vulnerable English border. Her daughter, Catholic claimant **Mary, Queen of Scots**, was married to the French Dauphin Francis and quartered the English royal arms with her own, openly asserting her dynastic claim to Elizabeth’s throne."
          ],
