                "text": "<p>In 1558 England had deep religious divisions between Protestants and Catholics. London and the south-east contained many Protestants, whereas northern and western parishes still preferred Catholic Latin Mass, and Marian bishops sat in the House of Lords. In May 1559 Elizabeth passed the Act of Supremacy as Supreme Governor and the Act of Uniformity with a new Prayer Book and twelve-pence fines.</p><p>England also faced the French threat in 1558 and early 1559. Having lost Calais, England faced French troops stationed in Scotland backing Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots. Elizabeth kept church ornaments and clergy vestments in parishes while peace talks continued with France in early 1559.</p>",
                "verdict": "Level 2 (6/12) — Accurate detail, but capped at 6/12 for sticking only to the two stimulus bullets. Add a 3rd own-knowledge point (e.g. keeping Philip II neutral or Elizabeth wanting bishops) and explain WHY."
