          "theme_heading": "A Fractured Realm: Marian Burnings, Geneva Exiles and the Search for Peace (1558–1559)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Upon her accession in 1558, Elizabeth inherited a deeply fractured nation scarred by decades of sudden Tudor religious upheaval. Conservative **northern gentry** and earls in the north and west remained devoted to traditional Roman Catholic Mass, images, and papal authority, whereas London, East Anglia, and south-eastern ports strongly favoured Protestant reform through continental trade links.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Following Mary I’s burning of nearly three hundred Protestants, zealous **Marian exiles** returned from Calvinist Geneva and Zurich demanding a stripped, scripture-only church purged of every Catholic remnant. Conversely, twenty-six conservative **Marian bishops** sat in the House of Lords opposing Protestant legislation, forcing Elizabeth to craft a cautious religious compromise to avert sectarian civil war.",
