          "content": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Passed by Parliament in May 1559, the **Act of Supremacy** permanently severed England’s legal ties with Rome and restored royal control over the Church. In a shrewd diplomatic gesture designed to calm Catholic consciences—and ease male anxieties over a woman heading the Church—Elizabeth adopted the title **Supreme Governor** rather than Henry VIII’s ‘Supreme Head’.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> The statute introduced a mandatory **Oath of Supremacy**, requiring all clergy, judges, university fellows, and royal officeholders to swear allegiance to Elizabeth’s spiritual authority or forfeit their posts. While twenty-six of twenty-seven Marian bishops refused and were deprived of their sees, roughly 8,000 ordinary parish priests took the oath, giving the new Protestant hierarchy peaceful continuity.",
          "paragraphs": [
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Passed by Parliament in May 1559, the **Act of Supremacy** permanently severed England’s legal ties with Rome and restored royal control over the Church. In a shrewd diplomatic gesture designed to calm Catholic consciences—and ease male anxieties over a woman heading the Church—Elizabeth adopted the title **Supreme Governor** rather than Henry VIII’s ‘Supreme Head’.",
            "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> The statute introduced a mandatory **Oath of Supremacy**, requiring all clergy, judges, university fellows, and royal officeholders to swear allegiance to Elizabeth’s spiritual authority or forfeit their posts. While twenty-six of twenty-seven Marian bishops refused and were deprived of their sees, roughly 8,000 ordinary parish priests took the oath, giving the new Protestant hierarchy peaceful continuity."
          ],
