          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Enacted alongside royal supremacy, the May 1559 **Act of Uniformity** established a single legal pattern of worship across every parish in England and Wales. It introduced a revised English **Book of Common Prayer** and required everyone to attend church every Sunday and holy day; absentees were classed as **recusants** and fined twelve pence (one shilling) per missed service.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> To win broad acceptance, the prayer book deliberately fused the 1549 and 1552 communion sentences so traditionalists could perceive Christ’s real presence in the bread and wine while Protestants understood it as a memorial meal. Meanwhile, the Ornaments Rubric retained traditional clerical vestments and church decoration, reassuring conservative rural congregations with familiar outward dignity.",
