          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> In the summer of 1559, William Cecil issued fifty-seven **Royal Injunctions**—detailed practical instructions guiding parish clergy on how to implement the statutory religious settlement in local communities. Every licensed minister had to preach royal supremacy at least four times a year, denounce papal superstition, and place a large **English Bible** alongside Erasmus’s *Paraphrases* inside every parish church.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> The injunctions outlawed traditional Catholic **pilgrimages**, ‘feigned’ miracles, candles burned before statues, and processions around monuments while permitting traditional hymns and kneeling at prayer for Christian reverence. Clerical marriage was permitted for Protestant priests, provided prospective wives produced formal certificates of good character approved by the diocesan bishop and two local Justices of the Peace.",
