          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> To police religious compliance nationwide, the Crown appointed 125 royal commissioners to conduct sweeping **visitations** across every English and Welsh diocese in autumn 1559, inspecting parish altars, removing superstitious images, administering the Oath of Supremacy, and licensing loyal preachers. Only around **400 clergy** resigned or were dismissed nationwide, allowing most rural parishes to transition smoothly under familiar local vicars.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Beyond Sunday liturgy, the established Church of England underpinned everyday village order, poor relief, and royal obedience. Ecclesiastical **Church courts** supervised community morality by prosecuting slander, adultery, sabbath-breaking, heresy, and disputed wills, while elected parish churchwardens collected alms and vicars read royal proclamations aloud from the pulpit to a largely illiterate rural population.",
