                "text": "<p>First, the Catholic nobility posed a serious problem for the religious settlement during the 1560s. Around a third of English nobles and gentry, especially powerful families in the north of England like the Earls of Northumberland and Westmorland, disliked Protestantism. Many northern Justices of the Peace ignored recusancy laws, sheltered former Marian priests in their manor houses, and eventually rose up in November 1569, holding Latin Mass in Durham Cathedral.</p><p>Second, the Puritan challenge came from radical Protestant clergy who wanted to remove Catholic-looking features from parish churches. Puritan bishops opposed keeping a silver crucifix in the Royal Chapel, and in 1566 thirty-seven London Puritan preachers lost their jobs after refusing Archbishop Parker’s Book of Advertisements over wearing the white surplice. Overall, I agree that the Catholic challenge was a greater threat than the Puritans.</p>",
