          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Radical Protestant reformers known as **Puritans** viewed Elizabeth’s 1559 settlement as a lukewarm compromise tainted by popish ceremony. Inspired by John Calvin’s Geneva, returning Marian exiles campaigned to strip parish churches of organs, stained glass, holy days, and silver **crucifix** ornaments—forcing Elizabeth to remove the silver crucifix from her own Royal Chapel when newly appointed Protestant bishops threatened to resign.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> At the **1563 Convocation** of clergy, Puritan reformers came within a single vote of abolishing kneeling at communion, saints’ feast days, and organ music altogether. Elizabeth regarded such clerical activism as a direct assault on her royal prerogative as Supreme Governor, insisting that once Parliament had settled worship, subjects owed silent obedience.",
