          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Domestic Catholic opposition initially remained subdued and largely passive during the early 1560s. Thousands of traditionalists acted as **Church Papists**, attending Anglican parish services on Sundays to escape the twelve-penny recusancy fine while hearing Latin Mass privately inside gentry manor houses whenever opportunity arose, especially across northern shires where sympathetic Catholic earls and lax local JPs ignored recusancy.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Elizabeth reinforced domestic calm by refusing to make ‘windows into men’s souls’ so long as Catholic subjects stayed politically loyal, while Pope Pius IV issued no immediate excommunication against her. By 1566–68, however, the militant European **Counter-Reformation** hardened attitudes: **Pope Pius V** forbade English Catholics from attending Protestant church services and William Allen founded the Douai seminary.",
