          "title": "Foreign Threats: France & The Netherlands",
          "theme_heading": "The Counter-Reformation Shadow: Alba's Tercios, Douai Seminaries and the Jesuit Mission (1562–1568)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Abroad, escalating European religious conflict reshaped England’s strategic security. When the **French Wars of Religion** erupted in 1562, Elizabeth briefly sent money and troops to aid French Huguenot Protestants in hopes of regaining Calais, though English defeat at Le Havre ended the venture under the 1564 Treaty of Troyes while keeping France too divided internally to threaten England.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Far graver strategic danger emerged across the narrow sea in the Spanish Netherlands. In 1567 Catholic King Philip II dispatched the ruthless **Duke of Alba** with 10,000 veteran Spanish troops to crush Dutch Protestant protests and heresy. Alba established the notorious **Council of Troubles** (‘Council of Blood’), executing thousands of Dutch Protestants and stationing a battle-hardened Catholic army just across the Channel.",
          "level_4": "Religious wars in France showed the danger of civil conflict, while in 1567 Philip II sent the Duke of Alba and 10,000 Spanish troops to crush Dutch rebels, placing an invasion army on England's doorstep.",
