          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> In November 1568, Anglo-Spanish friction escalated sharply when five Spanish vessels carrying **£85,000** in gold bullion borrowed from Italian bankers took shelter in Plymouth and Southampton from Channel storms and French privateers. The treasure—known as the **Genoese Loan**—was urgently bound for Antwerp to pay the Duke of Alba’s Spanish army operating in the Netherlands.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Advised by William Cecil to weaken Spanish military power in Flanders, Elizabeth audaciously impounded the bullion on the legal technicality that the gold still belonged to Genoese bankers until safely delivered to Antwerp, transferring the loan to the English Crown instead. Outraged, Alba seized English merchant ships and imposed a punishing **Antwerp trade embargo**, paralysing London cloth exports.",
