            "question": "3. ‘Mary, Queen of Scots’ claim to the English throne was the main problem in relations between Elizabeth and Mary in the years 1568–69.’ How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks]",
            "stimulus": [
              "Mary’s arrival in England (1568)",
              "The Catholic nobility"
            ],
            "bullet_points": [
              "Mary’s arrival in England (1568)",
              "The Catholic nobility"
            ],
            "model": "<p>Mary, Queen of Scots’ legitimate Catholic claim to the English throne was indeed the underlying root problem in relations between the two queens in 1568–69, because without that hereditary claim neither her arrival in England nor English noble conspiracies would have endangered Elizabeth’s crown.</p><p>First, Mary’s dynastic bloodline made her uniquely dangerous. As the granddaughter of Henry VIII’s elder sister Margaret Tudor, Mary possessed an unimpeachable legitimate Tudor pedigree, whereas Roman Catholics regarded Elizabeth as the illegitimate daughter of Anne Boleyn. Unlike Elizabeth, Mary had also given birth to an heir (James VI) in 1566. Consequently, from the moment she quartered the English royal arms, Catholic Europe and English traditionalists viewed Mary not merely as a neighbouring monarch, but as the rightful Catholic alternative waiting to replace Elizabeth.</p><p>Second, however, Mary’s sudden arrival in England in May 1568 turned a distant dynastic claim into an immediate constitutional and diplomatic crisis. Having fled Lochleven Castle after the murder of her husband Lord Darnley at Kirk o’ Field and her scandalous marriage to the Earl of Bothwell, Mary crossed the Solway Firth demanding English troops to crush Scotland’s Protestant regency under the Earl of Moray. Every option damaged Elizabeth: helping Mary risked deposing a friendly Protestant Scottish government; handing her back risked executing an anointed sovereign; freeing her risked her seeking French military aid. Even the 1568–69 York Inquiry over the eight Casket Letters could only manage—not solve—the crisis by issuing an inconclusive verdict to justify indefinite detention under the Earl of Shrewsbury.</p><p>Third, the ambition of England’s nobility and court factions turned Mary’s captivity into active conspiracy in 1569. Protestant courtiers including Robert Dudley secretly proposed marrying Mary to England’s premier nobleman, the Duke of Norfolk, to settle the succession, while Catholic Earls of Northumberland and Westmorland went further—planning to wed Mary to Norfolk, overthrow Cecil, and restore Catholicism by force.</p><p>In conclusion, I largely agree with the statement. While Mary’s flight to England in May 1568 and the scheming of English nobles triggered the immediate crisis of 1568–69, both only mattered because Mary possessed a powerful, legitimate Catholic claim to Elizabeth’s throne; without that bloodline claim, she would have been a powerless exile rather than a mortal magnet for rebellion.</p>",
