                "text": "<p>First, Mary’s arrival in England in May 1568 created an immediate problem for Elizabeth after Scottish Protestant lords forced Mary to abdicate and imprisoned her. Mary escaped from captivity and crossed into northern England asking her cousin Elizabeth for an English army to put her back on the Scottish throne. Elizabeth did not want to fight Scotland’s Protestant government or let Mary travel abroad to France, so she held Mary in guarded English castles.</p><p>Second, the Catholic nobility in England became restless once Mary was inside the country in 1568–69. Powerful traditionalist Catholic nobles in the north, such as the Earls of Northumberland and Westmorland, disliked Elizabeth’s Protestant government and preferred a Catholic monarch with a strong claim to the English throne. Overall, I agree that Mary’s claim to the throne was the main problem in relations between them.</p>",
