          "type": "narrative",
          "title": "Mary’s Claim to the English Throne",
          "theme_heading": "Two Anointed Queens: The Scottish Succession, Bloodline Rivalry and the French Pincer (1560–1565)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> In May 1568, the unexpected arrival of Catholic **Mary, Queen of Scots** in Cumbria confronted Elizabeth with an acute dynastic emergency. As the Catholic granddaughter of Henry VIII’s elder sister **Margaret Tudor**, Mary possessed an unimpeachable legitimate Tudor bloodline, making her the natural heir presumptive—and in strict Catholic eyes, England’s rightful reigning monarch.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Unlike unmarried Elizabeth, Mary had already given birth to a healthy male heir, Prince **James VI**, in June 1566 after marrying her cousin Henry Stuart, Lord Darnley. Even as a penniless fugitive stripped of her Scottish crown, Mary immediately became a dangerous political magnet for disaffected English Catholic nobles hoping to topple Cecil and restore Roman Catholicism.",
