          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Seeking to discredit Mary politically without putting a fellow anointed monarch on criminal trial, Elizabeth convened a formal commission of inquiry at **York** (later moved south to Westminster) between October 1568 and January 1569. Scottish Regent Moray dramatically produced eight French **Casket Letters** and love sonnets allegedly written by Mary to Bothwell proving she had plotted Darnley’s assassination.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Mary’s representatives condemned the letters as Protestant rebel forgeries and demanded she address Elizabeth face-to-face, which Elizabeth flatly refused. In January 1569 William Cecil delivered a calculated **not proven verdict**: neither clearing Moray of treasonous rebellion nor pronouncing Mary innocent, thereby giving Elizabeth a convenient diplomatic pretext to hold Mary captive indefinitely.",
