                "badge": "Printed Workbook WAGOLL",
                "text": "<p>The immediate trigger for Mary, Queen of Scots’ execution in February 1587 was her proven personal treason in the 1586 Babington Plot. Unlike the earlier Ridolfi (1571) and Throckmorton (1583) plots where Elizabeth could cling to doubts over Mary’s direct involvement, Catholic conspirator Anthony Babington planned for six gentlemen to assassinate Elizabeth while a foreign Catholic army invaded to free Mary. On 17 July 1586, Mary wrote back explicitly approving Elizabeth’s murder; under the 1584 Bond of Association and the 1585 Act for the Queen’s Safety, this signed endorsement of regicide made Mary legally liable to trial and execution at Fotheringhay Castle.</p><p>Second, Sir Francis Walsingham’s intelligence network and spies proved decisive in manufacturing irrefutable legal proof to overcome Elizabeth’s reluctance to execute an anointed monarch. Walsingham turned Catholic courier Gilbert Gifford into a double agent who intercepted every ciphered letter smuggled inside waterproof beer barrels at Chartley Manor, passing them to master codebreaker Thomas Phelippes. Once Phelippes decoded Mary’s fatal consent and forged a gallows postscript to expose Babington’s accomplices, Cecil and Parliament possessed unassailable evidence that forced Elizabeth to sign the death warrant.</p><p>Finally, the outbreak of open war with Spain after August 1585 made keeping Mary alive an intolerable national security risk. Since Pope Pius V’s 1570 bull Regnans in Excelsis had absolved Catholics from obedience to Elizabeth, Philip II was actively assembling an invasion Armada by 1586–87 to overthrow Protestant England. Privy Councillors warned Elizabeth that as long as Mary lived, foreign invaders and domestic Catholic plotters possessed a legitimate Catholic Queen waiting to take the throne; executing Mary in February 1587 permanently eliminated that domestic figurehead before Spanish warships could sail.</p>",
