          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> As Jesuit and seminary priests trained at Douai smuggled themselves into England after 1574—leading to Edmund Campion’s execution and £20 monthly recusancy fines in 1581—Secretary of State **Sir Francis Walsingham** uncovered the **Throckmorton Plot** in November 1583. Under torture, courier Francis Throckmorton confessed a plan for Mary’s cousin, the French Catholic Duke of Guise, to invade Sussex.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Crucially, Spain’s London ambassador Bernardino de Mendoza was financing the conspiracy with Spanish gold; Elizabeth expelled Mendoza, severing formal diplomatic ties with Madrid. To deter future plots, Cecil and Walsingham created the 1584 **Bond of Association** (enacted into law in 1585), binding signatories to execute anyone on whose behalf an assassination of Elizabeth was attempted.",
