            "workbook_badge": "In Printed Workbook • Page 5 (KT2 Booklet)",
            "question": "3. ‘Commercial rivalry was the main reason for rivalry between England and Spain in the years 1569–85.’ How far do you agree? Explain your answer. [16 marks]",
            "stimulus": [
              "Privateering and the activities of Drake",
              "Political and religious rivalry"
            ],
            "bullet_points": [
              "Privateering and the activities of Drake",
              "Political and religious rivalry"
            ],
            "model": "<p>While commercial rivalry and Francis Drake’s audacious privateering provoked bitter Spanish anger throughout the 1570s, political and strategic rivalry over the Spanish Netherlands—intensified by religious hostility—was ultimately the decisive reason Anglo-Spanish relations collapsed into war by 1585.</p><p>Commercial rivalry certainly poisoned relations between 1569 and 1581. Following Alba’s trade embargo on Antwerp after the 1568 Genoese Loan seizure, English merchants led by John Hawkins and Francis Drake sought to break Philip II’s strict imperial trade monopoly over the New World. Backed secretly by Elizabeth and court investors, Drake ambushed Spanish silver mule trains at Nombre de Dios in 1572 and raided the Pacific coast during his 1577–80 circumnavigation, seizing £140,000 from the Cacafuego alone and returning roughly £400,000 in bullion to Plymouth. When Elizabeth publicly defied Spanish ambassador Mendoza by boarding the Golden Hind at Deptford to knight Drake in April 1581, Philip II viewed Elizabeth as a state sponsor of piracy.</p><p>However, religious rivalry steadily transformed commercial friction into an ideological conflict. Devoutly Catholic Philip II saw himself as the champion of the Counter-Reformation, especially after Pope Pius V excommunicated Elizabeth in 1570. Spain backed Catholic conspiracies to put Mary, Queen of Scots on the English throne—promising 10,000 troops in the 1571 Ridolfi Plot and 200,000 ducats in the 1583 Throckmorton Plot—while Elizabeth executed Jesuit missionaries and sheltered Protestant Dutch ‘Sea Beggars’.</p><p>Above all, geopolitical security in the Netherlands proved the decisive trigger that turned cold war into open conflict. England could never tolerate a hostile Spanish army controlling deep-water Channel ports just miles from London. While Elizabeth preferred covert subsidies to Dutch Protestant leader William of Orange during the 1570s, two events in 1584 shattered the balance of power: William of Orange was assassinated in July 1584, and Philip II signed the Catholic Treaty of Joinville with the French Guise family in December 1584. Faced with total Dutch collapse and Catholic encirclement, Elizabeth signed the Treaty of Nonsuch in August 1585, sending 7,400 English soldiers to fight Spain directly.</p><p>Overall, I disagree that commercial rivalry was the main reason. Privateering for silver inflamed pride on both sides, yet Philip II tolerated Drake’s raids for over a decade without invading; it was the strategic and religious crisis in the Netherlands in 1584–85 that compelled England and Spain to go to war.</p>",
