                "level": "Level 2 (Stimulus-Bound Cap)",
                "text": "<p>Commercial rivalry caused major tension between England and Spain from 1569 to 1585 because Spain blocked English merchants from trading freely in the New World. Francis Drake raided Nombre de Dios in 1572 and then sailed around the world between 1577 and 1580 in the Golden Hind, capturing £140,000 of silver from the Cacafuego. Philip II was outraged when Elizabeth knighted Drake at Deptford in 1581.</p><p>However, political and religious rivalry also mattered. Philip II was a devout Catholic who hated Elizabeth’s Protestant Religious Settlement after the Pope excommunicated her in 1570. Spain supported the Ridolfi Plot in 1571 and the Throckmorton Plot in 1583 to put Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots on the throne. Overall, I disagree because religion caused more anger than money.</p>",
