          "theme_heading": "The Spanish Superpower: Philip II's Empire, the Dutch Revolt and English Sympathy (1570–1577)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Throughout the 1570s, Anglo-Spanish relations deteriorated steadily over religious, commercial, and strategic rivalry in the **Spanish Netherlands**. Because Low Countries Channel ports lay directly opposite the Thames estuary and Antwerp dominated English woollen cloth exports, Elizabeth dreaded Spanish absolutism crushing traditional Dutch provincial liberties and establishing a permanent Catholic military springboard facing southern England.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> When Dutch Protestants led by **William of Orange** rebelled against Alba’s harsh taxation and religious persecution, Elizabeth avoided open war while covertly sending secret loans, permitting English Protestant volunteers to fight, and briefly sheltering Dutch **Sea Beggars** before expelling them from English ports in 1572—an expulsion that ironically prompted them to seize Brielle and ignite general Dutch revolt.",
