          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Simultaneously, fierce commercial conflict erupted across the Atlantic as English **privateers** challenged King Philip II’s closed imperial monopoly over New World trade. After Spanish warships ambushed John Hawkins and **Francis Drake** at San Juan de Ulúa in 1568, Drake launched daring retaliatory **privateering** raids across the Spanish Main, ambushing silver mule trains at Nombre de Dios in Panama.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Rather than punishing her captains, Elizabeth secretly invested royal ships and capital into syndicates licensing legalised maritime plunder against Spanish shipping while issuing polite diplomatic denials to Madrid. After mutinous unpaid Spanish troops sacked Antwerp in the brutal **Spanish Fury** of November 1576, Elizabeth lent the Dutch Estates £100,000 under the Pacification of Ghent.",
