          "theme_heading": "The Deptford Knighting: Elizabeth's Defiance, Philip's Outrage and the £10,000 Reward (1580–1581)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Furious at Drake’s Pacific raids—and newly emboldened after annexing Portugal and its Atlantic war fleet in 1580—King Philip II instructed ambassador Mendoza to demand Drake’s immediate execution as a pirate and complete restitution of the captured treasure. Instead, in April 1581 Elizabeth boarded the *Golden Hind* at **Deptford** on the River Thames and publicly awarded **Drake’s knighthood** upon the quarterdeck.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Deliberately handing the ceremonial gilded sword to a visiting French royal diplomat while courting the Catholic **Duke of Alençon** as a marriage suitor and financing his Dutch intervention, Elizabeth signalled an anti-Spanish alignment with France. Publicly honouring ‘El Draque’ humiliated Philip II before all Europe, proving the English Crown endorsed maritime challenges to Spain’s empire.",
