            "model": "<p>Robert Dudley’s provocative actions in the Netherlands certainly deepened Philip II’s determination to defeat England, yet they were part of a wider strategic escalation in which Elizabeth’s Treaty of Nonsuch (1585), Mary Stuart’s execution (1587), and Francis Drake’s raid on Cadiz (1587) played equally decisive roles in turning tension into full-scale invasion by 1588.</p><p>On the one hand, Dudley’s actions in 1585–87 directly challenged Philip II’s sovereignty over the Spanish Netherlands. In January 1586, without Elizabeth’s permission, Dudley accepted the supreme title of ‘Governor-General of the Netherlands’, implying England intended to depose Philip II permanently as ruler of the Dutch provinces rather than broker a compromise peace. Although Dudley’s campaign suffered from chronic underfunding and English defections by Sir William Stanley at Deventer and Rowland Yorke at Zutphen in 1587, his garrison successfully defended deep-water Channel ports such as Ostend and Flushing—convincing Philip II that he could never reconquer the Dutch rebels until England itself was neutralized.</p><p>On the other hand, Elizabeth’s foundational decision to sign the Treaty of Nonsuch in August 1585 had already crossed the diplomatic point of no return before Dudley even set foot in Flushing. Financing 7,400 English troops to fight Philip’s commander, the Duke of Parma, amounted to an open act of war, prompting Philip II immediately to seize English shipping in Spanish ports and begin planning the ‘Enterprise of England’ in late 1585.</p><p>Furthermore, events in 1587 escalated hostilities to irreversible naval warfare. In February 1587, Elizabeth executed Catholic Mary, Queen of Scots, prompting Pope Sixtus V to promise Philip II one million gold ducats once Spanish troops landed on English soil. Two months later, in April 1587, Elizabeth sent Francis Drake to raid Cadiz harbour, where he humiliated Spain by destroying 30 vessels and burning seasoned oak barrel staves along the Algarve coast—infuriating Philip and locking Spain onto its 1588 Armada launch.</p><p>In conclusion, I only partly agree. Dudley’s acceptance of the Governor-Generalship insulted Spanish sovereignty and blocked Parma from deep-water ports, but it was a consequence of Elizabeth’s Treaty of Nonsuch—and it was Mary’s execution alongside Drake’s devastating raid on Cadiz in 1587 that drove Anglo-Spanish relations to all-out invasion in 1588.</p>",
