          "theme_heading": "The Treaty of Nonsuch: Leicester's Expedition and the Defense of Flushing (1585–1586)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Landing in the Netherlands in December 1585 with 7,400 men after England garrisoned the cautionary ports of **Flushing** and Brielle, **Robert Dudley**, Earl of Leicester, immediately enraged Elizabeth in January 1586 by accepting the supreme title **Governor-General** of the United Provinces without consulting her. Furious that accepting sovereignty openly repudiated Philip II’s hereditary rule, Elizabeth publicly humiliated Dudley before the Dutch.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Throughout 1586–87 Elizabeth deliberately starved the expedition of money, siege artillery, and reinforcements, hoping still to negotiate a compromise peace with Madrid while unpaid English troops suffered hunger and desertion. Even so, Dudley’s garrisons slowed the **Duke of Parma**’s coastal advance and held the deep-water port of Ostend, denying Spain an easy Channel anchorage.",
