          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Militarily and politically, Leicester’s Dutch campaign suffered demoralising blows. During the autumn 1586 skirmish outside **Zutphen**, celebrated Protestant courtier-poet Sir Philip Sidney was mortally wounded, and in January 1587 two Catholic English officers appointed by Leicester—Sir William Stanley and **Rowland Yorke**—treacherously surrendered the vital town of **Deventer** and Zutphen fort directly to Parma’s Spanish army.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Stanley and Yorke’s shocking defection shattered Dutch confidence in English loyalty, especially as rumours spread of secret Anglo-Spanish peace talks and English privateers disrupted Dutch Baltic shipping. Unable to cooperate smoothly with the merchant-led Dutch States-General or match Parma’s 30,000 veteran soldiers, a discredited Dudley was permanently recalled to England by Elizabeth in late 1587.",
