          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Learning from Walsingham’s spies in early 1587 that Philip II—spurred by Nonsuch and Mary Stuart’s execution—was assembling a vast invasion Armada, Elizabeth ordered Francis Drake to strike pre-emptively against Spanish ports. On 19 April 1587 Drake sailed twenty-four warships boldly into **Cadiz** harbour—Spain’s busiest naval arsenal—in a daring raid nicknamed **‘Singeing the King of Spain’s Beard’**.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Over three days of chaotic bombardment Drake’s gunners burned or sank **30 Spanish ships** and destroyed thousands of tonnes of naval provisions before raiding the Algarve coast and capturing the rich Portuguese spice carrack *San Felipe* (£108,000) off the Azores. Crucially, diverting Santa Cruz’s warships to hunt Drake delayed the Armada’s launch by an entire vital year until 1588.",
