          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Drake’s coastal raids off Cape St Vincent also inflicted hidden logistical ruin by burning huge stockpiles of seasoned **barrel staves** and hoops intended for the Armada’s food and water casks. Forced to manufacture hasty replacements from damp, unseasoned green pine timber, Spanish victuallers loaded leaky barrels that warped at sea, turning salted meat, ship’s biscuit, and drinking water foul.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Spanish naval command weakened further in February 1588 when Madrid’s most experienced fighting admiral, the Marquis of Santa Cruz, died suddenly in Lisbon. Philip II appointed the reluctant aristocratic **Duke of Medina Sidonia**—an able shore administrator who candidly protested his lack of sea combat experience and chronic **seasickness**—ordering him strictly to obey inflexible royal instructions.",
