          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> After violent spring storms forced emergency hull repairs at Corunna, the **130 ships** of Spain’s Great Armada carrying roughly 30,000 sailors and soldiers entered the English Channel at the end of July 1588. Philip II’s grand invasion strategy required Medina Sidonia to sail up the Channel to **Calais** and Flanders, link up with Parma’s 27,000 veteran troops, and escort their barges across to Kent.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Yet Philip’s rigid blueprint contained a fatal structural flaw: Parma controlled **no deep-water port** in Flanders, meaning his unarmed, flat-bottomed troop barges needed forty-eight hours of calm weather to emerge through shallow coastal canals blockaded by nimble **Dutch flyboats** under Justin of Nassau, while slow courier boats prevented Medina Sidonia and Parma from timing their rendezvous.",
