                "score": "6 / 12 Marks",
                "level": "Level 2 (Stimulus-Bound Cap)",
                "text": "<p>One reason for the English victory in 1588 was Spain’s flawed invasion plans. Philip II ordered the Duke of Medina Sidonia’s 130 ships to sail up the Channel and meet the Duke of Parma’s 27,000 soldiers in the Netherlands, even though Spain controlled no deep-water ports there and Dutch ships blockaded Parma’s barges in shallow canals.</p><p>Another reason was English ship design and tactics. John Hawkins built fast, agile race-built galleons armed with long-range culverins on four-wheel carriages. On 7 August 1588, Lord Howard and Francis Drake sent eight burning fireships into Calais, forcing Spanish captains to cut their anchors before English broadsides battered them at Gravelines.</p>",
