                "badge": "Printed Workbook WAGOLL",
                "text": "<p>Fatal weaknesses in Philip II’s Spanish invasion plans laid the foundation for English victory before a single shot was fired in the Channel. Philip’s rigid strategy required the Duke of Medina Sidonia’s 130-ship fleet to rendezvous off Dunkirk with the Duke of Parma’s 27,000 soldiers, yet Spain possessed no deep-water harbour in the Netherlands: Dutch Justin of Nassau’s ‘Sea Beggars’ blockaded Parma’s flat-bottomed barges in the shallow canals while messages between Sidonia and Parma took up to a week to arrive. Worse still, inexperienced commander Medina Sidonia replaced veteran Admiral Santa Cruz, while Drake’s 1587 Cadiz raid had burned seasoned barrel staves, leaving Spanish food rotting in leaky green-wood casks and cannons supplied with mismatched iron shot.</p><p>Second, superior English ship design and aggressive naval tactics shattered the Armada once it anchored helplessly off Calais. John Hawkins’ fast, low-profile ‘race-built’ galleons outperformed high-castled Spanish vessels designed for grappling and boarding, while English gun crews mounted long-range culverins on compact four-wheel carriages to fire rapid, destructive broadsides. Crucially, on the night of 7 August 1588, Lord Howard and Francis Drake sent eight burning, pitch-soaked fireships into Calais Roads, panicking Spanish captains into cutting their anchor cables and breaking their protective crescent formation so English broadsides could batter isolated ships at close range at the Battle of Gravelines on 8 August.</p><p>Finally, shifting weather—celebrated in England as the ‘Protestant Wind’—turned tactical defeat at Gravelines into total strategic catastrophe. Strong south-westerly winds prevented Medina Sidonia from turning back to collect Parma’s army, driving the damaged Spanish fleet north around uncharted Scotland and western Ireland into ferocious late-summer Atlantic gales. Because Spanish captains had cut their anchors to escape the Calais fireships, dozens of crippled galleons were driven onto Scottish and Irish reefs, ensuring only 67 shattered vessels ever returned to Spain.</p>",
