          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Sighted off the Lizard on 29 July 1588, Medina Sidonia’s Armada advanced up the English Channel in a tight seven-mile **crescent formation**, stationing heavy fighting galleons on its horned wings to protect slower supply hulks in the centre. Warping out of Plymouth against the wind, **Lord Howard of Effingham**, Francis Drake, and John Hawkins seized the windward weather gauge behind the Spaniards.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Over six days of running skirmishes past Plymouth, Portland Bill, and the **Isle of Wight**, agile English ships avoided Spanish grappling hooks and fired stand-off cannon salvos. Although few Spanish ships sank in the Channel, Howard prevented Medina Sidonia from seizing an anchorage in the Solent, herding the Armada onward to exposed Calais Roads on 6 August.",
