          "theme_heading": "The Bloodbath at Gravelines: Hawkins' Race-Built Galleons and Close-Range Broadside (1588)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> At dawn on 8 August 1588, the decisive nine-hour Battle of **Gravelines** erupted off the treacherous Flemish sandbanks. Closing to destructive cannon range of under a hundred yards aboard fast, low-built **race-built galleons** pioneered by Naval Treasurer John Hawkins, English squadrons repeatedly out-turned high-castled Spanish ships while shallow Flemish waters and Dutch flyboats barred Parma from coming out.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Mounted on compact four-wheeled naval gun-carriages, long-range English bronze **culverins** fired three times faster than cumbersome Spanish guns manned by soldiers trained primarily to fire one volley and board enemy decks. Relentless English broadsides sank or drove aground five major Spanish ships, inflicted over 1,000 casualties, and permanently ruined any chance of invading Kent.",
