          "title": "The Winds & Atlantic Retreat",
          "theme_heading": "The Protestant Wind: The Ordeal of the Scottish Isles and Shipwrecks on Irish Rocks (1588)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Blocked by the pursuing English fleet behind them and treacherous Flemish shoals ahead, a fierce south-westerly gale—celebrated across Protestant England as the **‘Protestant Wind’**—drove Medina Sidonia’s battered fleet north into the open North Sea on 9 August. Critically short of cannonballs, leaking badly, and drinking putrid water from green-wood barrels, the Armada had to limp home around Scotland and **Ireland**.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Uncharted North Atlantic autumn tempests turned Spain’s retreat into utter catastrophe: lacking spare heavy anchors cut at Calais and guided by inaccurate charts of the Gulf Stream, over twenty-five Spanish vessels wrecked along rocky Scottish and Irish coasts where thousands drowned or were killed ashore. Barely **67 ships**—roughly half the original fleet—straggled back to Spain with over 15,000 men lost.",
          "level_4": "Driven north by gale-force winds, the Armada was forced to sail around Scotland and Ireland. Lacking anchors, dozens of ships were wrecked in Atlantic storms, and only 67 ships limped home.",
