          "theme_heading": "The Protestant Deliverance: Elizabeth at Tilbury, Medals of Gold and English Triumph (1588–1589)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Even while the shattered Armada fled north, England remained braced against Parma’s army in Flanders; on 18 August Elizabeth rode in silver breastplate before 4,000 militia mustered by Leicester at **Tilbury** in Essex, famously declaring she possessed ‘the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too’. Victory reinforced Elizabeth’s personal authority despite her gender and strengthened English Protestant national pride.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Royal propaganda struck commemorative silver medals inscribed *Flavit Jehovah et Dissipati Sunt*—**‘God blew and they were scattered’**—portraying Protestant England as divinely favoured. Strategically, defeating the Armada preserved Dutch Protestant independence, confirmed English **naval prestige** across the Atlantic, and ended immediate danger of Spanish conquest even as costly war dragged on until 1604.",
