                "score": "8 / 16 Marks",
                "level": "Level 2 (Stimulus-Bound Cap)",
                "text": "<p>The theatre grew rapidly in London during Elizabeth's reign after James Burbage built The Theatre in Shoreditch in 1576, followed by The Curtain in 1577 and The Rose in 1587. Acting companies needed a noble patron such as the Earl of Leicester's Men so they were not arrested as vagabonds. Poorer groundlings paid one penny to stand in the open pit while wealthy gentry paid more to sit in covered galleries watching plays.</p><p>However, the nobility and lower classes also had separate traditional pastimes. Wealthy nobles hunted deer in private parks, practised hawking and fencing, and played real tennis to display their status. Meanwhile, lower-class farm labourers played violent village football matches with almost no rules. Overall, I agree that the theatre was the most significant feature because it grew very fast in London.</p>",
