          "isReversed": false,
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.1]</span> Sixteenth-century English education reflected humanist learning and Protestant belief that Christians should read the vernacular Bible, yet access remained sharply divided by class and gender. Overall **literacy** rose to roughly **30% of men** and **10% of women** by the end of Elizabeth’s reign—concentrated overwhelmingly among gentry, merchants, and yeomen rather than rural farm labourers, whose children worked from age six.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[1.2]</span> Boys of the middling sort attended elementary **petty schools** in a teacher’s home to learn basic English reading, writing, and arithmetic, while some girls attended **dame schools** focused on reading, sewing, and housework. Above all, Tudor schooling aimed to reinforce the **Great Chain of Being**, teaching every child obedience to God, monarch, and social superiors.",
