          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> The greatest educational expansion occurred in fee-paying town **grammar schools**: forty-two were founded in the 1560s and thirty more in the 1570s by wealthy Protestant benefactors and merchant guilds, replacing dissolved monastic schools. Attended by bright boys aged ten to fourteen from gentry, professional, merchant, and yeoman families (plus talented scholarship boys), school days ran from 6 a.m. to 5 p.m.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Grammar schools taught a demanding **classical curriculum** centred on **Latin** and Greek authors such as Cicero, Virgil, and Seneca alongside public rhetoric, scripture, archery, and strict corporal punishment supervised by student monitors. Meanwhile, merchants’ and craftsmen’s sons without Latin followed petty-to-apprenticeship routes learning practical book-keeping, commercial arithmetic, and skilled urban trades.",
