          "isReversed": true,
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Secular Elizabethan drama blossomed after the 1572 Vagabonds Act required touring actors to secure a nobleman’s licence—producing troupes like Leicester’s Men and the **Queen’s Men** (1583). In 1576 actor **James Burbage** built England’s first permanent purpose-built playhouse, **‘The Theatre’**, in Shoreditch outside City of London jurisdiction, soon followed by the Curtain (1577) and the Rose (1587).\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Performances drew up to 2,000 spectators across every class: poorer **groundlings** paid just one penny to stand in the open pit around the thrust stage while wealthy gentry paid extra for cushioned gallery boxes or seats upon the stage itself. Conversely, **Puritans** and London aldermen denounced theatres for spreading bubonic plague, attracting pickpockets, and encouraging sinful idleness.",
