            "model": "<p>Economic pressures—such as price inflation, falling real wages, cloth trade slumps, and agricultural enclosure for sheep farming—played a major role in impoverishing Elizabethan labourers, yet sustained demographic population growth was the underlying root catalyst that made every economic shock so severe between 1558 and 1588.</p><p>On the one hand, economic and agricultural pressures directly destroyed rural jobs and purchasing power. Because raw wool and cloth accounted for roughly 80% of England’s exports, profit-seeking landowners enclosed open village strip-fields with hedges and converted arable crop land into pasture for sheep farming, which required a tiny fraction of the workforce. Evicted tenant farmers and landless cottagers lost common grazing rights while rack-renting landlords sharply raised entry fines and rents. Simultaneously, sudden foreign trade disruptions—such as the Duke of Alba’s 1568 Antwerp cloth embargo—threw thousands of East Anglian and West Country spinners and weavers out of work overnight, while earlier Tudor coin debasement fuelled price inflation as real wages stagnated.</p><p>On the other hand, rapid population growth from roughly 3 million in 1551 to nearly 4 million by 1588 was the fundamental structural driver behind rising poverty and vagabondage. With hundreds of thousands more mouths to feed and stagnant farming yields, demand for grain and housing permanently outstripped supply—quadrupling bread prices whenever consecutive bad harvests struck in the mid-1550s, 1573–77, and 1586. Surplus rural youths unable to inherit land flooded into towns like London and Norwich looking for work, swelling the ranks of wandering ‘masterless men’ who terrified magistrates.</p><p>Furthermore, long-term institutional changes left destitute families without a safety net: Henry VIII’s earlier Dissolution of the Monasteries had abolished traditional church charity and hospital alms, forcing jobless families onto the roads until Parliament introduced compulsory poor rates in 1572 and wool work-stocks in 1576.</p><p>In conclusion, I agree to a large extent because inflation, rack-renting, cloth embargoes, and sheep enclosure were the immediate mechanisms that evicted tenants and crushed wages. However, those economic pressures were inseparable from relentless population growth: without a million extra people competing for scarce jobs, land, and grain, neither bad harvests nor enclosure would have produced mass vagabondage on such a national scale.</p>",
