                "score": "8 / 16 Marks",
                "level": "Level 2 (Stimulus-Bound Cap)",
                "text": "<p>Sheep farming and enclosure caused serious rural poverty during Elizabeth's reign. Because English wool was very profitable to export, wealthy landowners fenced off open village fields and common land with hedges so they could keep large flocks of sheep instead of growing arable crops. Looking after sheep needed far fewer farm labourers than ploughing and harvesting grain, and landlords also raised rents sharply through rack-renting, which evicted tenant families onto the roads.</p><p>At the same time, England's population grew rapidly from around three million to nearly four million people by 1588. Having far more people meant there were not enough jobs, houses, or food supplies to go around, so bread prices rose steeply and landless young men wandered towards towns like London. Overall, I agree that economic pressures were the main reason because enclosure directly took away farm workers' jobs.</p>",
