          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Elizabethan society divided the poor into two distinct moral and legal categories: the **impotent poor** (or deserving poor—young orphans, widows, the elderly, sick, and disabled physically unable to work, who merited sympathetic parish charity) versus **sturdy beggars** (the idle or undeserving poor—able-bodied adults suspected of choosing criminal idleness over honest daily labour).\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Public anxiety over wandering masterless men was whipped up by Kent magistrate **Thomas Harman**’s popular 1567 illustrated book *A Caveat for Common Cursitors*, which exposed underworld tricks such as ‘counterfeit cranks’ chewing soap to fake epilepsy and ‘Tom o’ Bedlams’ feigning madness. Lacking any salaried police force, gentry JPs feared vagrant bands spread bubonic plague, violent crime, and **rebellion**.",
