          "isReversed": true,
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> By mid-decade MPs recognised a crucial shift in social attitudes: recurring cloth-trade slumps meant many able-bodied poor genuinely could not find work. The **1576 Poor Relief Act** therefore instructed town and parish officials to purchase parish stocks of **raw wool**, flax, iron, and hemp so willing unemployed residents could spin yarn and earn an honest wage.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Those able-bodied vagrants who still stubbornly refused parish work were sent not to ordinary county gaols, but to newly established houses of correction known as **Bridewells** (named after London’s Bridewell Palace), where inmates endured strict hard labour to reform their habits. Together, the 1572 and 1576 statutes established state responsibility for unemployment underpinning the 1601 Poor Law.",
