      "prologue": "Across the Tudor countryside, the roads were stalked by desperate families in rags. A catastrophic surge in population, recurring harvest failures, and soaring inflation pushed thousands of landless labourers to the edge of starvation. Meanwhile, ambitious landlords enclosed open fields to graze profitable sheep, tearing down cottages and driving peasants into the towns. Terrified of vagabond riots and crime, Elizabethan society underwent a profound revolution in thinking: transitioning from medieval charity and savage branding irons to England's first nationwide system of state-enforced welfare. Why did poverty and vagrancy escalate so rapidly in Elizabethan England, and how did the government's approach shift from punishment to state welfare?",
