            "model": "<p>First, Drake’s circumnavigation (1577–80) was financially and commercially transformative because of the vast haul of captured Spanish treasure brought back to Plymouth in September 1580. Having raided Spanish Pacific ports like Valparaiso and captured the treasure galleon Cacafuego carrying £140,000 of silver and jewels alone, the Golden Hind returned roughly £400,000 in bullion—delivering a staggering 4,700% profit to court syndicate investors. Elizabeth’s personal royal share exceeded her entire annual Crown income, enabling her to pay off England’s foreign debt while encouraging wealthy London merchants to fund joint-stock companies such as the Levant Company (1581).</p><p>Second, the voyage dramatically elevated England’s reputation as a global sea power and inspired early colonial ambition. Despite losing four of his five ships, surviving violent gales in the Strait of Magellan, and crossing the uncharted Pacific and Indian Oceans, Drake returned as only the second captain in history—and the first Englishman—to sail around the world. Furthermore, in June 1579 Drake landed on the Californian coast and claimed ‘Nova Albion’ (New England) for Queen Elizabeth, demonstrating that Philip II’s Pacific monopoly could be breached and inspiring Sir Walter Raleigh’s subsequent Virginia expeditions.</p><p>Finally, the circumnavigation had major diplomatic significance by sharply escalating Anglo-Spanish hostility toward war. Furious at English plunder in waters claimed exclusively by Spain, ambassador Mendoza demanded Drake’s execution as a pirate; instead, Elizabeth publicly boarded the Golden Hind at Deptford in April 1581 and knighted Drake on deck. This calculated royal defiance humiliated Philip II, proved Elizabeth backed maritime aggression against Spain’s empire, and deepened the strategic rivalry that culminated in war in 1585.</p>",
