          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> Simultaneously, English royal shipyards transformed vessel engineering. Replacing slow, top-heavy medieval carracks built primarily as floating castles for boarding soldiers, Royal Navy Treasurer John Hawkins developed sleek **race-built galleons** from the 1570s: longer, narrower hulls with lowered forecastles and stern castles reduced wind drag, improved stability in Atlantic gales, and carried larger water and food stores for oceanic voyages.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> Combining large square main sails for downwind speed with triangular **lateen sails** on the mizzenmast for agile windward tacking, these galleons mounted heavy bronze **culverins** and demi-culverins along full-length gun decks. Capable of pounding enemy hulls from long range and reloading rapidly, race-built warships gave English explorers both oceanic endurance and formidable firepower.",
