          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Economic necessity drove English merchants to seek distant markets after the Antwerp cloth trade collapsed and Spanish embargoes choked traditional wool exports. Wealthy London investors pooled risk in royal chartered joint-stock companies: the **Muscovy Company** (1555) traded furs and naval timber with Russia, the Eastland Company (1579) entered the Baltic, and the **Levant Company** (1581) imported Ottoman spices and silks.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> Others hunted direct sea routes to Asia to bypass Iberian monopolies and acquire African gold, ivory, and enslaved cargoes (pioneered by John Hawkins in 1562). Between 1576 and 1587 **Martin Frobisher** and John Davis braved Arctic icebergs seeking a Northwest Passage above Canada, charting Baffin Island while Humphrey Gilbert promoted North American settlement.",
