          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.1]</span> Drake’s three-year **circumnavigation** of the earth (1577–80)—only the second in world history and the first led throughout by an English captain—delivered sensational financial, political, and psychological rewards. Returning roughly £400,000 in Spanish treasure yielded syndicate investors a **4,700% profit**, while Elizabeth’s Crown share exceeded an entire year’s royal income and cleared England’s national debt.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[5.2]</span> Gathering priceless Pacific rutters and charts, signing spice treaties in the Moluccas, and proving English galleons could survive a global voyage boosted national maritime confidence and inspired Raleigh’s colonial schemes in Nova Albion’s wake. Conversely, Elizabeth’s public **Deptford knighting** of Drake in April 1581 shattered Spain’s exclusive claim to the Pacific and hastened open Anglo-Spanish war.",
