                "score": "8 / 16 Marks",
                "level": "Level 2 (Stimulus-Bound Cap)",
                "text": "<p>Sir Walter Raleigh played a vital role in organising the attempted colonisation of Virginia after Queen Elizabeth granted him a six-year royal patent in March 1584. Although Elizabeth forbade her favourite courtier from sailing in person, Raleigh sent Amadas and Barlowe on a scouting voyage in 1584, named the territory Virginia in the Queen's honour, brought Manteo and Wanchese to London, and raised £40,000 from investors to equip the 1585 expedition.</p><p>Rivalry with Catholic Spain also drove English colonisation plans in the mid-1580s. As relations with Philip II worsened toward open war in 1585, English leaders wanted a North American base near the Caribbean so privateers could attack Spanish treasure ships and challenge Spain's New World empire. Overall, I disagree that trade was the main reason because rivalry with Spain and Raleigh's ambition mattered much more.</p>",
