                "text": "<p>The drive to expand trade and acquire raw materials untied to European rivals was a powerful economic motive behind the attempted colonisation of Virginia in the 1580s, yet strategic rivalry with Catholic Spain—championed by Sir Walter Raleigh and Richard Hakluyt—was equally crucial in winning royal backing from Queen Elizabeth.</p><p>On the one hand, commercial pressures strongly encouraged English merchants and courtiers to establish an American colony. Since the collapse of the Antwerp cloth market and Spanish trade embargoes had exposed England’s dangerous over-reliance on European wool exports, geographer Richard Hakluyt’s 1584 Discourse on Western Planting argued that North America offered both a captive market for English woollens and a direct source of exotic commodities—sugar, wine, naval timber, pitch, hemp, and hoped-for gold or copper—without paying high duties to Spanish, Mediterranean, or Baltic middlemen. Colonisation also promised employment for England’s growing population of landless poor while friendly Algonquian trade scouted in 1584 suggested rich commercial rewards.</p><p>On the other hand, geopolitical and religious rivalry with Philip II’s Spain gave Virginia urgent strategic purpose by 1584–85. As Anglo-Spanish tension slid toward open war under the Treaty of Nonsuch (1585), Elizabeth and Walsingham backed Virginia primarily because Roanoke offered a permanent North American naval base from which English privateers could ambush Spanish treasure fleets sailing up the Florida Channel from the Caribbean—draining Philip II’s war chest while challenging Catholic domination of the New World.</p><p>Finally, neither commercial nor anti-Spanish motives would have produced an expedition in 1584–87 without the personal ambition and organisation of Sir Walter Raleigh. Granted a six-year royal patent by Elizabeth in March 1584 after his half-brother Humphrey Gilbert drowned, Raleigh invested £40,000 of his own and court backers’ wealth, flattered Elizabeth by naming the territory ‘Virginia’, brought Manteo and Wanchese to London so Thomas Harriot could learn Algonquian, and organised both the 1585 Grenville/Lane military venture and John White’s 1587 family colony.</p><p>In conclusion, I agree to a substantial extent, because long-term trade independence from Europe underpinned merchant investment and Hakluyt’s blueprint. However, trade cannot be separated from anti-Spanish strategy: in the crisis years of 1584–88, Elizabeth approved Raleigh’s venture above all because Virginia promised to enrich English commerce while simultaneously striking at Spain’s Atlantic treasure lifeline.</p>",
