          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.1]</span> For the April 1585 colonising expedition, Raleigh recruited **107 colonists**—exclusively men—aboard seven vessels commanded at sea by his fiery, aggressive cousin **Sir Richard Grenville**, with veteran army officer **Ralph Lane** appointed colony governor from ashore alongside mathematician-scientist Thomas Harriot and watercolour artist John White (whom Harriot helped learn Algonquian from Manteo and Wanchese in London).\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[2.2]</span> From the very outset, recruitment imbalances and divided leadership proved deeply flawed: roughly half the 107 settlers were professional soldiers and status-conscious gentlemen expecting quick gold mines or Indigenous tribute rather than skilled **farmers**, carpenters, stone-masons, and winter fishermen able to make an Atlantic wilderness colony self-sufficient, while Grenville and Lane clashed bitterly over strategy.",
