          "theme_heading": "Shoals and Spoiled Corn: The Tiger Wreck, Food Shortages and Secotan Hostility (1585–1586)",
          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.1]</span> Departing Plymouth on 9 April 1585—already too late in spring to plant crops for an autumn harvest—the expedition suffered disaster on 29 June 1585 when flagship **Tiger** struck a sandbar at Ocracoke Inlet. Flooding seawater ruined almost all the hold’s **seed corn**, salt, dried peas, and gunpowder, leaving the garrison unable to grow harvests or preserve meat.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[3.2]</span> When Grenville sailed back to England in August for fresh supplies, Lane’s 107 men at Roanoke became dangerously dependent on local Algonquians for maize and woven reed **Secotan fish traps** they could neither build nor repair themselves, while brought-over European diseases (measles or smallpox) sparked mysterious deaths and deep suspicion inside Native villages.",
