          "text": "<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.1]</span> Relations with the Secotan people deteriorated rapidly over winter 1585–86. In July 1585 Grenville had already burned the Native village of Aquascogoc over a missing silver cup; by spring 1586, exhausted by feeding unproductive English soldiers during a severe regional drought, Secotan ruler **Chief Wingina** (who took the war-name Pemisapan) stopped trading maize and coordinated local resistance.\n\n<span class=\"para-ref\">[4.2]</span> Suspecting an imminent confederate attack, Governor Ralph Lane staged a ruthless pre-emptive **1 June 1586 ambush** at Dasamonquepeuc, luring Wingina to a peaceful parley and beheading him. Surrounding Algonquian tribes cut off all food supplies; facing starvation before Grenville’s delayed relief ships arrived, the colonists abandoned Roanoke on 18 June 1586 aboard **Francis Drake**’s passing fleet.",
